{
  "caveats": [
    "POST-HOC and IN-SAMPLE: the rules were designed after seeing the A6 cause taxonomy (wrapper object, free-text required argument, near-duplicate tools, other) and are scored on the same items. These numbers are not out-of-sample performance.",
    "The label is open-labeller DISAGREEMENT on required arguments: a noisy proxy for underspecification, not ground truth. A disputed item can be well specified (a labeller error) and an agreed item can be underspecified (shared bias).",
    "The control set is stratum-matched, not a random sample, and the 50/50 class balance is by construction. Precision does not transfer to a real prevalence of underspecified tasks; recall and specificity are the transferable quantities.",
    "Structural confound: every disputed item has at least one required argument (disagreement on required arguments needs one), while a share of controls has none. Argument-based findings cannot fire on those controls, which inflates specificity and precision. The `restricted_to_items_with_ge1_required_arg` table removes this confound and is the one to read.",
    "Possible circularity: the `near_duplicate_tool` field is a boolean that matches a threshold of about 0.70 on `max_cosine_to_other_tool`, a cosine similarity between the gold tool and the most similar other tool in the same catalog; the `primary_cause` and `all_causes` fields are present for disputed items only. The tool-pair near-duplicate findings here use name edit distance and description token overlap on the same catalogs, so the near-duplicate result is not independent of how that field was derived. How `primary_cause` was assigned was not inspected beyond the field values.",
    "No launch claim: nothing here gates, scores or changes a published number. The check is opt-in, ADVISORY only."
  ],
